      "writtenOn": "2022-12-28T19:18:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Maybe add a comment here that we aren\u0027t explicitly declaring uint stages for add, sub, mul, cmpeq, and cmpne because the `int` equivalents can operate on unsigned (by converting to/from signed)?\n\nFor multiply it maybe worth noting in a comment that we expect the hardware representation to produce the same result for signed and unsigned multiplication. I don\u0027t know when that would not be the case, except for the higher bits of a widening multiplication afaik (which I\u0027m guessing we\u0027re not worried about here?).",
      "revId": "2a5a6f767ae81d32af473dcf1674a89cff69c477",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "830e8525_309b75a1",
        "filename": "src/opts/SkRasterPipeline_opts.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 3360,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-28T19:27:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Signed and unsigned multiplication are always the same result in the low N bits (e.g. the low 16 bits of `uint16*uint16` are the same as `int16*int16`), but can vary in the higher bits. This isn\u0027t an issue for us; the SkSL result of an N-bit integer multiply is an identically-typed N-bit result. This matters if you want to multiply `int32*int32` and produce a 64-bit result, but that\u0027s not something we support.",
      "parentUuid": "15d1555b_bd5a93b7",
      "revId": "2a5a6f767ae81d32af473dcf1674a89cff69c477",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
